        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Classic exercism book-store problem. Prices are in cents. Implement a DP over counts (counts of 5 books, each 0..=max). Standard approach: sort counts in descending order, and use DP to decide which books to include in each group.\n\nSimplest and most robust approach: memoized recursion over the count vector [c1..c5], where each step picks a subset of books with count > 0, subtracts 1 from each, and adds the cost of the group. But the state space is large. Better approach: sort counts in descending order, and use the known optimal structure: the optimal grouping can be found by DP over the sorted counts.\n\nStandard solution: sort counts in descending order, and use DP over the index of counts. Actually, the well-known approach: since counts are sorted in descending order, groups can only differ in size up to the count of the 5th book. Use memoized recursion: state = (sorted counts). At each step, pick k from 1..=min(5, number of non-zeros), and take one from the k largest counts. Since counts are sorted, the choice is deterministic: take from the top k. This is the known optimal greedy-with-DP approach.\n\nLet's implement it:\n\n```rust\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\npub fn lowest_price(basket: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    let mut counts = [0i32; 5];\n    for &b in basket {\n        counts[b as usize - 1] += 1;\n    }\n    counts.sort_unstable_by(|a, b| b.cmp(a));\n    let mut memo = HashMap::new();\n    fn cost(n: usize) -> i32 {\n        match n {\n            1 => 800,\n            2 => 1520,\n            3 => 2160,\n            4 => 2560,\n            5 => 3000,\n            _ => unreachable!(),\n        }\n    }\n    fn rec(counts: &[i32], memo: &mut HashMap<[i32;5], i32>) -> i32 {\n        if counts.iter().all(|&c| c == 0) { return 0; }\n        if let Some(&v) = memo.get(counts) { return v; }\n        let mut best = i32::MAX;\n        for k in 1..=5 {\n            if counts[k-1] == 0 { break; }\n            let mut next = *counts;\n            for i in 0..k { next[i] -= 1; }\n            next.sort_unstable_by(|a,b| b.cmp(a));\n            best = best.min(cost(k) + rec(&next, memo));\n        }\n        memo.insert(*counts, best);\n        best\n    }\n    rec(&counts, &mut memo)\n}\n```\n\nWait — is it optimal to always take from the top k? Known result: yes, when counts are sorted in descending order, the optimal solution takes one from the top k books for some k. This is a standard solution (e.g., the canonical solution for the exercism Rust track). Actually, the canonical solution does exactly this: sort counts in descending order, and at each step try k=1..=5, taking from the first k. This is proven optimal.\n\nThe signature of lowest_price — the test calls `lowest_price(input)` with `&[1]` (i32). Good.\n\nCheck src/lib.rs.\n",
